 "design": "R6 how many runs per configuration the interval needs before it can be trusted.\n\nDesign fixed before running (2026-09-16, written 13:15 EDT). DataDecide ships three runs per\nconfiguration, and every coverage number in this paper inherits that three. The cross-half covariance\ndivides by the number of runs minus one, so two runs leave a single degree of freedom per configuration\nand the estimator's variance has to climb. A practitioner who can afford only two runs, or who can\nafford six, gets no guidance from anything we have measured, and snap-r9-runs answers a different\nquestion, which is how many runs a decision rule needs rather than whether the interval covers.\nThis run sweeps the runs per configuration over 2, 3, 4, 6 and 10 on the margin-like population at 4,000\nreplicates each, holding everything else at the shipped design. It scores the wild bootstrap-t and the\ncluster-robust t under both clusterings on every replicate and records the median width beside the\ncoverage, so a reader can see what a fourth run buys and what a second run costs. Seed 20260957 with a\ncell offset of five hundred keeps the draws distinct.\n",
